<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para"><strong>High salary courses for BiPC students are the ones where pay keeps growing after the first job: a life-science degree with a data skill, pharmacy aimed at drug safety, nursing or physiotherapy with a growth plan, and the long routes of dentistry and MBBS.</strong> None of them pays a high salary quickly.</p>
  <p class="lead-para">In the pay samples below, the top of the range for the jobs these courses lead to is about Rs 4.5 to 7.5 lakh a year after four years, so the skills you add beside the degree decide how far pay goes. Those early choices keep higher-income options open and can bring financial freedom closer.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Answer:</strong> Pay can grow furthest where a skill sells beyond one employer. A life-science degree with a data skill leads on growth, though its pay data is thin, followed by pharmacy with a safety or regulatory skill, biotechnology or food engineering, nursing and physiotherapy. MBBS and BDS pay late, and medical coding is a short, crowded route.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision filter:</strong> For every course ask: what must I clear to get a seat, when does pay really move, and which second skill moves it?</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> For your top two courses, find five live job posts and the college's full fee list. Then compare both with a tenth of your family's education budget.</li>
